  "summary": "Rumelt’s strategy kernel has three parts: a diagnosis of the key challenge, a guiding policy for addressing it, and coherent actions that implement the policy.",
  "summary_citation_ids": ["S1", "S7"],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "The three components",
      "content": "1. **Diagnosis:** Explain the situation and identify the important challenge or problem. 2. **Guiding policy:** Set out how you will deal with that challenge. 3. **Coherent actions:** Specify connected actions that put the guiding policy into effect. Rumelt says all three must be present; if any is missing, it is not really a strategy.",
      "citation_ids": ["S1", "S7", "S3"]
    },
    {
      "heading": "What makes the actions strategic",
      "content": "Actions should reinforce one another and be few enough to maintain focus—not an expansive list of priorities. As an example, Rumelt describes Microsoft adapting to AI: diagnose the challenge, adopt a policy of investing in a major leader and incorporating the technology into its search engine, then take coherent actions to carry that out.",
      "citation_ids": ["S1", "S2", "S5"]
    }
  ],
  "missing_topics": []
}

{
  "coverage": "complete",
  "insufficient_evidence": false,
  "summary": "April Dunford says to identify what a customer would do if your product did not exist: include both the status quo and any alternatives that make the buyer’s shortlist, not just products that look like yours. In B2B, status quo can be manual work or tools like spreadsheets, and “no decision” is a meaningful competitor.",
  "summary_citation_ids": ["S1", "S2", "S3"],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Identify what you must beat",
      "content": "Ask, “If we didn’t exist, what would a customer do?” Dunford calls the answers your competitive alternatives. Include two groups: (1) the status quo—the company’s current way of trying to solve the problem, even if it is poor; and (2) the other options that land on the buyer’s shortlist if they decide to purchase something different. Don’t limit the list to products that resemble yours.",
      "citation_ids": ["S1", "S2"]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Count status quo and indecision",
      "content": "Status quo might mean a spreadsheet, pen and paper, interns, or manual labor. Dunford warns against discounting it: she says B2B businesses lose about 40% of deals to “no decision,” which can mean the buyer stays with the existing approach. Her positioning advice is to treat both that status quo and shortlisted alternatives as things you need to beat to win the deal.",
      "citation_ids": ["S2", "S3"]
    }
  ],
  "missing_topics": []
}

