{
  "coverage": "complete",
  "insufficient_evidence": false,
  "summary": "Kohavi warns that misleading A/B results can come from sample-ratio mismatches, repeatedly checking results and stopping once they look significant, or treating a nominal p-value as the chance a finding is false.",
  "summary_citation_ids": ["S1", "S2", "S4", "S6"],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Sample-ratio mismatch",
      "content": "If a test designed to split users 50/50 produces a materially different split, that is a red flag: the experiment may be wrong and its results should not be trusted. Kohavi gives the example of a million-user test split 50.2/49.8; a statistical check might show that such a result would occur by chance only about once in half a million experiments. Possible causes include bots interacting with control and treatment at different rates, data-pipeline problems, skewed traffic filtering, or campaigns sending users into an experiment partway through the site. Microsoft found about 8% of experiments had this mismatch.",
      "citation_ids": ["S1", "S2", "S3"]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Repeatedly checking p-values and stopping when significant",
      "content": "Using real-time p-value monitoring to stop an experiment as soon as it becomes significant inflates the false-positive (type I error) rate. Kohavi says a nominal 5% error rate could become roughly 30% with this approach.",
      "citation_ids": ["S4", "S5"]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Misreading statistical significance",
      "content": "A p-value is not the probability that the result is false; it is calculated assuming the null hypothesis is true. The false-positive risk also depends on the prior likelihood that the tested idea succeeds. For Airbnb search, where the success rate was 8%, Kohavi gives a 26% chance of a false positive even for a statistically significant result with p < 0.05.",
      "citation_ids": ["S6"]
    }
  ],
  "missing_topics": []
}

