  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "He did not fire every product manager",
      "content": "Chesky clarified that he had not gotten rid of the people; his point was about changing how people worked together. He said he made the group much smaller and reassigned many product managers as program managers, training many of them for those roles.",
      "citation_ids": ["S1", "S3"]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Why he changed the model",
      "content": "Chesky described a pattern in which, as he became less involved, projects had more spin, less-clear goals, weaker advocacy, fewer resources, and moved more slowly. He said he later got back into the details and reviewed product and marketing work on a regular cadence. The evidence supports a shift in the operating model—not a mass firing—as his response.",
      "citation_ids": ["S2", "S3", "S4"]
    }
  ],
